#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175818 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/id #uuid "7520ab43-c5b9-541b-80a9-4af1b91b2428" :question/math-academy-id "e-8855" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=3y-y^2$ and $x=y,$  as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7e/7eca2a7eecfb9555a7e1555555a6d7b435a794e5715537d4fe94998ef00d124a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=3y-y^2$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/86/861a1300845e3832a201dd00410d462350f33a4654332b6b6fa7140dc6476dbb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=3y-y^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([3y-{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{16}{3}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175819 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "9f7f3606-5c49-51d0-b4e5-cffd0310b6c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8857" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=2-y^2,$ $x=y,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown below. Using the shell method, find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/16/16a30b8310128aa588b3278b5a0984193ce57cbb5ea9118fc167a636487363b8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=2-y^2$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/f6/f69e2c1cd3b099a4ba192357db32274867c4f296e2c34e31e5110ee31b270317.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=2-y^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15608928-4e7f-5b0d-a6ce-90c1e1a5b6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2y-{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539522} {:answer/id #uuid "67cda087-075b-574c-aace-9ef713972155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y({y}^{2}-2y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539649} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc07ce1-b348-5d8f-acd3-7b08c3d96c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{({y}^{2}-4y)}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539740} {:answer/id #uuid "a3066621-d9af-51db-9c80-ef7f8e379a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}y(4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539753} {:answer/id #uuid "b712480b-490e-5af1-9fec-c822b73444ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b712480b-490e-5af1-9fec-c822b73444ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539801} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ba66103-f7c3-5909-8944-c611bc72d320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b61e8b5a-f6d1-5f01-ad3a-3725586d9405" :question/math-academy-id "q-106122" :question/problem "![](images/30/30aa4c5e72919d09a0a2323dc8af90a73810047857f3c89fe9c3005a10c195ae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/cb/cbfd6c409590e6b6d350b5ae8f6531944467e90991b17582febe228a6a8dfdb2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e540eb0-8cac-5747-b4e7-b7ccd316406a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539509} {:answer/id #uuid "19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539533} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2d1a27-b068-55ec-bdb4-71d7924738b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539592} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c54165-081f-572b-8aab-18d7b5b1b30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539782} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b025ce-348b-58ed-829c-e108dbb26644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539533} :answer-field/id #uuid "eefd7b42-cc63-5201-b954-24eada1a35de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1404f19f-22e0-51b4-954a-73312901eafa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107142" :question/problem "![](images/92/920aca572a3b6fc828d6c37246b348aa2b017d1bc4e022ea06c12d0fa09ad6ee.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=(1-y)^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{1-x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{1-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-x \\\\ x & =1-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=1-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=(1-y)^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =(1-y)^{2} \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/02/02e6b4204b1edf682dd332e28830dc84aa0e41d3728c66442d777f6fe9daccfc.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=(1-y)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[(1-y)^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006652b3-463c-58d3-a96f-cbc2e068d0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y({y}^{3}-2y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539478} {:answer/id #uuid "0a54eb67-bb5d-5e42-97b8-8eefbb20b3ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y({y}^{3}-y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539498} {:answer/id #uuid "73e752f4-3838-5b2c-ae9b-87e14a752cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539673} {:answer/id #uuid "80a9bc50-5c65-5ff2-8cbc-ddd7ad27ca94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539691} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d3b21-f148-548e-a6ef-acd9b0a583ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80a9bc50-5c65-5ff2-8cbc-ddd7ad27ca94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539691} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b6c840-af69-50a7-90ae-fcfa0390abc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ede84af-72eb-557a-81fe-42dab11d2478" :question/math-academy-id "q-107218" :question/problem "![](images/89/8957a7db313379597a21e6b51d6c4493058af207a2d8edd5fafb8e549ba45aae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1+{y}^{3},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1+{y}^{3}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1+{y}^{3} \\\\ y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y(1-y)(1+y) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/b9/b91dedd1282ce1e9d8546bc6f7c52eba8741a2cd49613a03ef62866a9ba91b0c.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1+{y}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1+y]-[1+{y}^{3}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1853b3af-28f4-5ef9-aad8-03a6eaf50446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539528} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee05a02-1be6-5002-8a40-86020d83d578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539743} {:answer/id #uuid "aedf06dc-11bf-5210-87fb-2324f78a6574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539777} {:answer/id #uuid "e847bcfd-c659-5ed7-a215-e4e98478c99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539872} {:answer/id #uuid "efd08d18-ffa2-5e0a-8532-bcddc6c9bf2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1853b3af-28f4-5ef9-aad8-03a6eaf50446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539528} :answer-field/id #uuid "410a3349-db09-5c6c-877b-7b43454d7322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e2fc7b4-11f3-5f1c-807e-b40bd8df74a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-107304" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5eac1b1d21378d64b90fc2463eb7664b30224594ba5d63aa70a7c412097012a0.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(3-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/84/84e62b99598649ac48d55171b0d6bf7e5d26e38a6ad94c46ef9c89145e60e29a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y([1+y]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y(3y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({3\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (3(\\frac{27}{3}-0)-(\\frac{81}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139fc7af-0867-5a86-940c-8281935a937b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539518} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7b25f4-6ce8-527a-8907-b4e941ca805d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539569} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3a483b-b14e-5eaf-a6fa-5d488f2042e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539665} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fa5ee1-55ae-551e-b55c-cb55db8dd6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(1+{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539758} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9016d5-be3d-5277-aaaf-8658739447a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f3a483b-b14e-5eaf-a6fa-5d488f2042e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539665} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7ab163e-5d5d-5698-b7ab-03eea7c74f9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dabe85e-85d9-5f83-8489-ec7933e9dac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126191" :question/problem "![](images/da/dadb1e7740ee89fbbf9cffe69501ebd7be655ec9aaee30071b09fc767e80092c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+2y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=1+2y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+2y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-y)(1+y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/d9/d9f7cf87c59b7a0fc9600178795524f35a72b12466385e63eafa35c1fdb3537c.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+2y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1+2y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1+2y-{y}^{2}-2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c110bb-5316-5161-b14e-6b363f107559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-y-\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539645} {:answer/id #uuid "75eaed8c-a198-5c3a-b426-50603d367304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(1-\\frac{1}{2}y-\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539676} {:answer/id #uuid "7f54ff01-aed0-58e9-858f-625c9907586d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}y+1)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539689} {:answer/id #uuid "89944f1d-1e72-5cb7-b5b1-8e207883c50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}y-1)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539709} {:answer/id #uuid "b4882823-b7fc-57ca-8289-748a54539d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+y-2)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89944f1d-1e72-5cb7-b5b1-8e207883c50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}y-1)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539709} :answer-field/id #uuid "0601c164-985b-5dd4-912d-b59e95bef8da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0ffc976-87a0-51e6-8ff5-4a0a8b70546a" :question/math-academy-id "q-126325" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b61c628a012f6bde3144254b521e11f8803e089cd7f83f2c19a1255cf22c0e3a.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}},$ $x=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ and $g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+1 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}{y}^{2}+y+3 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2}-4y-12 & =0 \\\\ (5y+6)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/12/122a21e72dc7473cc34ed3ece0325294eadcc86cbe8a5b91e4dfe4be77b369c0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}y+1])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}y-1)\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bdd21a9-b33a-5551-894f-eefc01cd484b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539503} {:answer/id #uuid "36e02b44-a709-533e-8923-9cd6635295c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539580} {:answer/id #uuid "54fb0fe4-d278-59fe-baf1-7676faf2662c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539625} {:answer/id #uuid "96c8069d-b163-5345-aa21-42e30dfc170b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539729} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29541b-ac6a-5c52-b43d-f88aae4b76d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36e02b44-a709-533e-8923-9cd6635295c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539580} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac10175-16bb-5872-9c8f-0de12b255415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e40037f0-3304-53a5-a4f4-7220f338664f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126377" :question/problem "![](images/48/482781bb21d011df5a9140b2b1fb0ebe1108f746c0055077d4f4e9a91fce735d.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=\\sqrt{2-x},$ $x={y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-x \\\\ x & =2-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)={y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/48/48905a174d688fdb3e099debb7840173a2ce047816003a5be9c089997196f9fb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=f(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f4675ff-30c4-572a-8b6c-5272dc8a8aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(6y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539541} {:answer/id #uuid "3383ee3a-fd4a-5273-a400-128ba9a72cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y{(2{y}^{2}-4y)}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539575} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc3c7d3-e229-5ae0-8d8b-3f3ed9e9f05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539748} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5199d3-7358-5a1e-8adf-9222be3fa0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2{y}^{2}-4y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539804} {:answer/id #uuid "ff744269-4ea5-571c-8ec4-ce039a7674e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(4y-2{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fc3c7d3-e229-5ae0-8d8b-3f3ed9e9f05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539748} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab089e7c-7f52-5582-ac6f-752c4f8bf60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2289657c-a9c3-56eb-97a8-f3d9729a0b73" :question/math-academy-id "q-17023" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5a3a246f2ecb4bee6cfc46f34d14b5abc9fcf22ed0d6173ed465bfab2cfeca6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $x=y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 5y-2{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/d8/d873c0acb12392e4ea40f761c72c1903a91c4f0e9fef04b586a71697957e2401.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([5y-2{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7553f952-2b67-5919-b814-110becde50fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7553f952-2b67-5919-b814-110becde50fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539674} :answer-field/id #uuid "b70140cd-8dd2-5535-a3c9-379587f1fc13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efbcafae-ea63-52c2-855e-a9cfa22be6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352199" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d8d3dfa1134b7fd5861ff70e493ead2227dd2d322e8436e28a903e0c596a408.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2},$ $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the second function in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)={y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 4y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/c5/c5a117e2af85e3380942939812442eac7b899a2259af79ad032654fc5662a767.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({4\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-2(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{32}{3}-8) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9bfb4c-8588-57d6-bd1d-a486a788d455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a9bfb4c-8588-57d6-bd1d-a486a788d455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539656} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c63fd60-a918-5151-ad1a-213063aeef6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f03e4c2d-a256-544b-8a7f-59a90059d73b" :question/math-academy-id "q-352372" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcb67e2d3fe9a413c02bb00b6d8cf7c0ac8825db1d360ab883f08baa3b6c2e9c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=7-y-{y}^{2},$ $x=5-{y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Now, let $f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=5-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 7-y-{y}^{2} & =5-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2-y & =0 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/cf/cfb914bf7ed0a0c6b9f784d46a6761cd97c352fcc4e82abb29a72dc12d8ab7ca.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=5-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([7-y-{y}^{2}]-[5-{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{4}{2}-0)-(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
